`kinetic::kinetic_typography` is red on `main` in the FreeBSD job (runs [37920890588](https://github.com/storytold/effectcraft/actions/runs/37920890588) at 4aaadd0 and [37925646049](https://github.com/storytold/effectcraft/actions/runs/37925646049) at d5f78f3, both `crates/automation/tests/qa/kinetic.rs:78:5: time remap 0.3 s → 1.6 s of the precomp`), and green on other commits, which is the tell: the assertion sits on the noise floor.

The assertion is not wrong about the product. Instrumenting it on a pristine `origin/main` shows the frame it wants is in fact the closest one — 1.0751 against the precomp at 1.6 s, and 13–20 against every other time on the precomp's timeline:

| time on the precomp | mean diff against the parent at 0.3 s |
| --- | --- |
| 0.3 (the remap ignored) | 16.95 |
| 1.5 | 17.69 |
| **1.6** | **1.0751** |
| 1.7 | 17.62 |
| 1.9 (clamped) | 17.64 |

The 1.0751 is 10% of the pixels, concentrated in thin outlines along the glyph edges, values mostly under 16/255: a nested precomp and the precomp rendered on its own do not produce bit-identical text anti-aliasing, and how close they get varies by platform, by runner and by font stack. `1.0` is simply below that noise floor, so the check flips with unrelated changes. The suite already knows this: same-path determinism is asserted at `1e-9` (`lifecycle.rs`), while the other cross-path comparison allows `< 8.0` (`compositing.rs`, "composites like the stage").

So this asks the question the check means, instead of a pixel tolerance: the expected time has to be the closest one by a clear margin, with an absolute bound of 8.0 for the "same frame" half.

